Q: PCF Control build error - [pcf-1014] [Error] Manifest validation problem: instance requires property &quot;manifest&quot; A: <p>Your manifest file must start with a manifest element like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>
&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;manifest&gt;
  &lt;control
    namespace=&quot;RR&quot;
    constructor=&quot;AzureMapsAddressAutofill&quot;
    version=&quot;1.0.1&quot;
    display-name-key=&quot;Azure Maps Address Autofill&quot;
    description-key=&quot;Azure Maps Address Autofill PCF&quot;
    control-type=&quot;standard&quot;&gt;
    ...
  &lt;/control&gt;
&lt;/manifest&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>With a valid manifest and after running</p>
<pre><code>npm run refreshTypes
</code></pre>
<p>the interfaces `IInputs` and `IOutputs` are generated.</p>
<p>For further reading, here ist the manifest documentation: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/component-framework/manifest-schema-reference/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/developer/component-framework/manifest-schema-reference/</a></p>

Q: How is Meyers&#39; implementation of a Singleton actually a Singleton A: <blockquote>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>static Singleton&amp; instance()
{
    static Singleton s;
    return s;
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>By itself, your example isn't a singleton. Or rather, it's unclear if it is one without seeing the definition of <code>Singleton</code>.</p>
<p>The <code>Singleton</code> class needs to have a private constructor to be considered a singleton, and indeed the original example by Meyers has that (see &quot;Scott Meyers - More Effective C++&quot;, Item 27: &quot;Limiting the number of objects of a class&quot;).</p>
<p>A singleton type by definition needs to resist having more than once instance of it getting created, which is usually achieved by having a private constructor (and a function that returns the only instance).</p>
<hr />
<p>Under the hood, this can very roughly be considered to be equivalent to the following C++98 (and might even be implemented vaguely like this by a compiler):</p>
<pre><code>static bool __guard = false;
static char __storage[sizeof(Singleton)]; // also align it

Singleton&amp; Instance() {
  if (!__guard ) {
    __guard = true;
    new (__storage) Singleton();
  }
  return *reinterpret_cast&lt;Singleton*&gt;(__storage);
}

// called automatically when the process exits
void __destruct() {
  if (__guard)
    reinterpret_cast&lt;Singleton*&gt;(__storage)-&gt;~Singleton();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The thread safety bits make it get a bit more complicated, but it's essentially the same thing.</p>
<p>Looking at an actual implementation for C++11, there is a <em>guard variable</em> for each static (like the boolean above), which is also used for barriers and threads.  Look at Clang's AMD64 output for:</p>
<pre><code>Singleton&amp; instance() {
   static Singleton instance;
   return instance;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The AMD64 assembly for <code>instance</code> from Ubuntu's Clang 3.0 on AMD64 at -O1 (courtesy of <a href="http://gcc.godbolt.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://gcc.godbolt.org/</a> is:</p>
<pre><code>instance():                           # @instance()
  pushq %rbp
  movq  %rsp, %rbp
  movb  guard variable for instance()::instance(%rip), %al
  testb %al, %al
  jne   .LBB0_3
  movl  guard variable for instance()::instance, %edi
  callq __cxa_guard_acquire
  testl %eax, %eax
  je    .LBB0_3
  movl  instance()::instance, %edi
  callq Singleton::Singleton()
  movl  guard variable for instance()::instance, %edi
  callq __cxa_guard_release
.LBB0_3:
  movl  instance()::instance, %eax
  popq  %rbp
  ret
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that it references a global guard to see if initialization is required, uses <code>__cxa_guard_acquire</code>, tests the initialization again, and so on.  Exactly in almost every way like version you posted from Wikipedia, except using AMD64 assembly and the symbols/layout specified in the <a href="http://refspecs.linux-foundation.org/cxxabi-1.83.html#guards" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Itanium ABI</a>.</p>
<p>Note that if you run that test you should give <code>Singleton</code> a non-trivial constructor so it's not a POD, otherwise the optimizer will realize that there's no point to doing all that guard/locking work.</p>

Q: Nuxt js 3 cookies not set in the hosted environment A: <p>When you call <code>getCookie</code> immediately after <code>setCookie</code>, you are trying to read something that has not reached the browser yet. Cookies are sent to the client in the response and only come back on the next request. Because of that, <code>getCookie</code> only checks the incoming request and returns <code>undefined</code> or <code>{}</code> if the request did not already contain that cookie.</p>
<p>What likely happened locally is this:</p>
<ol>
<li>Your browser still had an old cookie from a previous run.</li>
<li>The incoming request included that stale cookie.</li>
<li><code>getCookie</code> read the old value, not the one you just set.</li>
<li><code>deleteCookie</code> did not help here because it only affects the response, not the already parsed request.</li>
<li>Seeing a value made it look like the new cookie was working.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the hosted environment, the session was probably clean. There was no incoming cookie, so <code>getCookie</code> correctly returned nothing.</p>
<p>You should not read the cookie to verify it at this point. You already have the correct value in memory. Just use that.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// server/api/login.post.ts
export default defineEventHandler((event) =&gt; {
  const token = &quot;set by login api&quot;;

  // Optional cleanup of old cookies
  const cookies = parseCookies(event);
  for (const cookieName in cookies) {
    deleteCookie(event, cookieName);
  }

  setCookie(event, &quot;pathumToken&quot;, token, {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: true,
    sameSite: &quot;none&quot;,
    path: &quot;/&quot;,
  });

  // getCookie reads the incoming request only.
  // Do not use it here.

  console.log(&quot;Set Cookie =&quot;, token);

  return {
    token, // Return the value you already have
  };
});
</code></pre>
<p>One more important point. You are using <code>secure: true</code> with <code>sameSite: &quot;none&quot;</code>. This requires HTTPS. If your app is behind a proxy like Nginx or Cloudflare and TLS is terminated there, make sure the server correctly detects HTTPS. Otherwise the browser will silently reject the cookie.</p>
